(Ord. No. 750, § 2, 6-19-18)

1053:
(a) A motor vehicle that has been wrecked may be removed by a law enforcement officer from highways, rights-of-way, and public property, when requested by the owner or when such motor vehicles are in such a condition that they constitute a health hazard or unsightly nuisance.
(b) An illegally parked vehicle may be removed by a law enforcement officer from public property within the city limits under any one of the following conditions:
(1) The vehicle is obstructing traffic;
(2) The vehicle is blocking a private or public entrance;
(3) The vehicle is blocking a fire hydrant;
(4) The vehicle has been reported stolen or taken without the consent of the owner;
(5) The vehicle has no registration plate;
(6) The vehicle has been illegally parked in an area clearly and adequately marked tow-away zone;
(7) The vehicle has been abandoned for 72 consecutive hours;
(8) The driver of the vehicle is incapacitated by injury or illness;
(9) The vehicle has been illegally parked in a fire lane;
(10) The vehicle has been illegally parked in a handicapped accessible parking spot;
(11) The driver fails to produce proof of minimum insurance, and the officer confirms that the vehicle is uninsured;
(12) The driver is arrested for driving under the influence of alcohol, and no other person is available and capable of driving the vehicle;
(13) The driver has accumulated five or more parking citations that have not been paid;
(14) Any condition which, in the reasonable judgment of the investigating officer, presents a traffic hazard or a threat to public safety;
(15) Any condition otherwise authorized by state law or outlined in other sections of this Code; or
(16) The vehicle has been left or abandoned overnight in a city park when the city park is closed.
(c) Under the above conditions, the chief of police or any city law enforcement officer shall have the authority to take possession of motor vehicles or parts of motor vehicles located on public streets, rights-of-way, highways, and/or public property. The vehicle's owner and/or driver may request their towing service, or the investigating officer may request that the towing service currently under contract with the city impound the vehicle. If the vehicle is unattended, the vehicle will be towed by the towing service currently under contract with the city.
(Ord. No. 827, 11-21-23)

1058:
(a) The police chief, traffic engineer, or a designee thereof, upon approval by ordinance or resolution of the mayor and council, is authorized to designate and maintain with appropriate traffic control signs, markings and devices, after engineering and traffic investigations:
(1) Speed zones;
(2) Truck routes and streets to be designated specifically to prohibit various classes of trucks;
(3) One-way streets, no left turn, no right turn, and no U-turn;
(4) Freight loading zones and regulations;
(5) Parking and no parking zones and regulations thereon;
(6) Pedestrian crosswalks;
(7) Other safety zones for pedestrians; and
(8) Traffic lanes; or
(9) Providing for any other sign, marking or zone necessary for orderly and safe conditions on the streets of the city.
(b) The police chief, or a designee thereof, shall cause to be placed and maintained traffic control signs, signals and devices when and as required under this chapter and other traffic ordinances of the city to make effective the provisions of this chapter and these ordinances. The police chief, or a designee thereof, may cause to be placed and maintained such additional traffic control devices as deemed necessary to regulate traffic under this chapter and other traffic ordinances of the city or under state law, or to guide or warn traffic.
(c) All traffic control signs, signals, devices and markings shall conform to specifications in the manual on uniform traffic control devices adopted by the state transportation board. All signs and signals required under this section for a particular purpose shall, so far as practicable, be uniform as to type and location throughout the city. All traffic control devices so erected and not inconsistent with the provisions of state law or this Code shall be official traffic control devices of the city.
(d) The police chief shall maintain or cause to be recorded a current schedule of all traffic rules, regulations and orders set forth in subsection (a) of this section, which record shall be available for inspection by the public. For items listed in subsection (a) of this section, this schedule and any amendments thereto shall become effective only upon approval thereof by ordinance or resolution of the mayor and council. This schedule is hereby incorporated in this section, and copies thereof shall be available for public inspection in the office of the city clerk.
(e) Any violation of any traffic zone, marking, sign or other traffic control device established under this section shall be a violation of this Code.
(Code 1982, § 10-1-3; Ord. No. 627, pt. I, 12-21-10)

1070:
(a) No person shall stop, stand or park a vehicle, except when necessary to avoid conflict with other traffic or in compliance with law or the direction of a police officer or traffic-control device in any of the following places:
(1) On a sidewalk;
(2) In front of or within three feet of a public or private driveway, or in such manner as to block ingress or egress thereof;
(3) Within an intersection;
(4) Within 15 feet of a fire hydrant;
(5) Within any area marked as a coach or bus stop;
(6) Within 30 feet of the approach to any flashing beacon, stop sign or traffic-control signal located at the side of a roadway;
(7) Between a safety zone and the adjacent curb or within 30 feet of points on the curb immediately opposite the ends of a safety zone, unless the city manager and chief of police have indicated a different length by signs or markings;
(8) Within 20 feet of the driveway entrance to any fire station and on the side of a street opposite the entrance to any fire station within 50 feet to such entrance (when properly signposted);
(9) Alongside or opposite any street excavation or obstruction when stopping, standing, or parking would obstruct traffic;
(10) On the roadway side of any vehicle stopped or parked at the edge or curb of a street;
(11) At any place where official signs prohibit stopping;
(12) A space of 30 feet in front of each entrance of any hotel, theater, school or church;
(13) At any curb marked red or yellow by direction of the city manager and chief of police;
(14) At any place prohibited by the city manager and chief of police and indicated by official signs or markings;
(15) Between the front building restriction line and the front property line except on a bona fide paved or graveled driveway.
(b) No person shall move a vehicle, not lawfully under his control, either into any such prohibited area or more and leave such vehicle away from a curb such distance as is prohibited by this article.
(c) No person shall park at an angle any vehicle 24 feet or more in length on any street or roadway in the city.
(d) No person shall park any vehicle in violation of limitations indicated by official signs, including, but not limited to, duration limitations during specified hours.
(Ord. No. 382, 10-8-93; Ord. No. 560, 11-21-06; Ord. No. 827, 11-21-23)

